  offerDialogue:
    name: Professor Bhan
    sentences:
    - The road ends here because the canyon separates the settlement from the land
      on the other side.
    - That shop belongs to one of the <b><#E09500>local merchants</color></b>.
    - Right now, people have to take a long route around the canyon just to reach
      it.
    - 'The <b><#E09500>Shopkeeper</color></b> commissioned a bridge to create a direct
      connection to Canyonville.

'
    - 'This will be your <b><#E09500>first real project</color></b>.

'
    - Remember what you've learned.
    - Consider the <b><#E09500>structure</color></b>, the <b><#E09500>loads</color></b>
      it will carry, and how your design will support them.
    - During training, I showed you exactly what to build.
    - This time, the <b><#E09500>decision is yours</color></b>.
    - You may use a <b><#E09500>beam</color></b> or <b><#E09500>truss design</color></b>.
    - Study the site, review the <b><#E09500>required load</color></b>, and choose
      the structure you believe is appropriate.
  continueOfferDialogue:
    name: Professor Bhan
    sentences: []
  reminderDialogue:
    name: Professor Bhan
    sentences:
    - This is your <b><#E09500>first real contract</color></b>, {PlayerName}.
    - You already know the basics from your training.
    - Now it's time to <b><#E09500>apply what you've learned</color></b>.
    - You may choose the <b><#E09500>bridge design</color></b> you believe is most
      suitable for the site.
    - Just make sure your bridge connects both sides and meets the <b><#E09500>contract
      requirements</color></b>.
    - The <b><#E09500>construction site</color></b> is just ahead.
    - Once you're ready, head to the <b><#E09500>Blueprint</color></b>.
    - That's where you'll begin construction.
  finishedContractDialogue:
    name: Professor Bhan
    sentences:
    - Well done.
    - You successfully connected the shop to Canyonville.
    - More importantly, you <b><#E09500>made the decision yourself</color></b>.
    - 'You chose your approach, built the structure, and tested your design.

'
